      "claim": "In a DFS of a connected undirected graph, every edge that is not in the DFS tree is a back edge connecting a vertex to one of its ancestors in the DFS tree.",
      "evidence": "Standard DFS property for undirected graphs: when an edge (x,y) is examined, if y is already visited it must lie on the current recursion stack, i.e., be an ancestor of x; otherwise the edge would become a tree edge. No cross or forward edges exist.",
      "weight": 0.30,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "For a non‑tree edge (u,v) with pre(u) < pre(v), u is the ancestor of v in T.",
      "evidence": "By step 1 the edge joins an ancestor–descendant pair. The vertex discovered earlier (smaller preorder number) is the ancestor, so u is the ancestor of v.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "If u is an ancestor of v, then the lowest common ancestor of u and v is u.",
      "evidence": "Definition of lowest common ancestor: the deepest vertex that is ancestor of both. When one vertex is ancestor of the other, that ancestor is the deepest common ancestor.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "For an ancestor u and descendant v, post(v) < post(u).",
      "evidence": "In a postorder traversal a vertex is recorded after all vertices in its subtree have been recorded; therefore a descendant finishes before its ancestor.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Statement I (post(u) < post(v)) is false, while statements II and III are true.",
      "evidence": "From step 4 we have post(v) < post(u), contradicting I. Steps 2 and 3 give truth of II and III respectively.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [2,3,4]
    }
  ],
